  "verbalization": "Cephalexin, a broad-spectrum antibiotic, is implicated in the development of arthropathy through a chain of indications that link it to bacterial arthritis and ultimately to arthropathy. The evidence suggests that Cephalexin is associated with bacterial arthritis, which is a known indication for Cefalotin and Cefonicid, both of which are also linked to arthropathy. This creates a pathway where Cephalexin, through its indication for bacterial arthritis, contributes to the development of arthropathy via these intermediate agents. The consistent scores across the three paths indicate a strong, albeit indirect, relationship between Cephalexin and arthropathy, likely mediated by the bacterial arthritis pathway. The mechanism appears to involve the systemic effects of Cephalexin on the immune system or inflammatory processes, which may lead to arthropathic changes. While the exact mechanism remains unclear, the integrated evidence supports a plausible causal link through the indicated chain of indications.",
